  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">El Obeid displaced and host community Livelihood Recovery project</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The ongoing conflict in South Kordofan has resulted into flow of populations to neighbouring North Kordofan for safety, livelihood and basic services. This influx of IDPs together with the existing displaced in NK due to the drought and famine since 1980 is creating pressure on the accommodating host families, community facilities and the general economy. According to the El Zakat Chamber, by the start of 2013 more than 8000 families were hosted in North Kordofan State, with 1600 families accommodated in out skirts of El Obeid city-  namely in Hay Tadamon, Hay Typa and Hay Sharief. These numbers have significantly changed since the major conflict between the GOS and SRF forces in Abu Kersholla (South Kordofan) which show displacement of at least 38,000 men, women and children from SK to NK. CAFOD has been supporting emergency education projects in 4 schools that fall in the outskirts of El Obeid with specially high burdens of displaced populations taking refugee among host communities and sharing the scarce resources available. The target locations lack basic services such as electricity, security, transportation and potable water. They are approximately 6-5 kilometres from the city main economic opportunity spots. Women and men depend on offering unskilled labour and performing petty trade petty trading to earn a living. They work as house helps or labourers in an already overstretched economy.  
The current project will target both host and hosted community based on the level of need as the key criteria. CAFOD and national partners SIDO and Nuba Mountains Organization will work with two communities according to the result of a survey done by SIDO and the Diocese of El Obeid with close support of CAFOD. The project will also work very closely with the Ministry of Social welfare (NK State) as well as the NK HAC Commissioner&apos;s Office. The very poor, the elderly and other vulnerable are targeted and their special needs will be addressed accordingly. The project will thus target conflict affected families, drought/famine displaced as well as corresponding hosts families with needs.
CAFOD and its national Partner SIDO will work together to address identified problems and support households find solution according to existing capacity and coping mechanism. A participatory Hazard Capacity and Vulnerability Analysis (HVCA) will be conducted and identified priority actions funded and supported as appropriate.</narrative>
  </description>
